diff --git a/tools/lean-bridge/SCHEMA.md b/tools/lean-bridge/SCHEMA.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..2bd63967822797b4ffb509468d102bd5b25a631c
--- /dev/null
+++ b/tools/lean-bridge/SCHEMA.md
@@ -0,0 +1,189 @@
+# Trusted Lean core export, version 1
+
+This is a local evaluation artifact, not an admitted production importer. The
+command accepts only the seven authored fixture IDs. `exportSource` is a generic
+library API for **trusted** source strings; it must not be connected to uploads,
+contestant projects, an AI response, or arbitrary dependency files without a real
+untrusted-code sandbox. Elaboration can execute code. Neither `--trust=0`, resource
+limits, a changed working directory, nor this fixture allowlist is an OS sandbox.
+
+`CoreExport.lean` walks actual checked `ConstantInfo` values and `Expr` constructors.
+It does not parse mathematical meaning using source regexes, rename a theorem to
+an axiom, generate substitute proofs, or decide LOGOS acceptance. `sorry` and added
+axiom dependencies are preserved and must not become checked claims. The actual
+`sorry` fixture exceeds the 32-declaration closure budget: its resource-failure
+envelope retains `sorryAx`, marks the closure incomplete, and emits no partial graph.
+
+## Exact envelope
+
+Each command writes one compact JSON object and one terminal newline. Exported
+facts return process code 0; Lean elaboration errors and export/resource failures
+return 1. A wrong argument count or unknown fixture ID returns 64 with the separate
+`macsad-lean-command-error/1` object, never an apparent core export.
+
+The core object's exact keys are:
+
+| Key | Type and meaning |
+|---|---|
+| `schema` | Exact string `macsad-lean-core/1` |
+| `exporterVersion` | Exact string `1`; outer evidence also hashes the exporter source/module |
+| `compiler` | Object with `version` and `gitCommit`, both strings read from the running Lean runtime |
+| `source` | Object with `fixtureId`, `fileName`, `byteLength`, `utf8Hex` |
+| `status` | `exported`, `elaboration-error`, `resource-limit`, or `export-error` |
+| `diagnostics` | Array of objects with exactly `severity` and `message`; severity is `information`, `warning`, or `error` |
+| `targetName` | Structured Name described below |
+| `imports` | Array of structured direct-import Names from the actual environment |
+| `declarations` | Referenced declaration closure; the requested target is element zero |
+| `nodes` | Ordered core-node records described below |
+| `axiomDependencies` | Array of exact Names from Lean's own transitive `collectAxioms` |
+| `closureComplete` | Boolean; true only when the bounded closure walk finished |
+
+`source.fixtureId` and `source.fileName` are strings. `byteLength` is a nonnegative
+JSON integer. `utf8Hex` is lowercase hex of the exact UTF-8 source actually supplied
+to Lean, **not** a digest claimed by an input header. It is null only when source
+size exceeds the explicit budget; that failure is not admissible evidence. The
+outer mechanical harness must compare these bytes to its requested source and
+compute SHA-256 itself. Original source, exporter, compiler, imported `.olean`
+artifacts and JSON require separate retained hashes. This evaluation retains the
+fixture/exporter/compiler/JSON hashes, but not a complete imported-library closure
+manifest yet. Module names alone are not
+cryptographic import provenance.
+
+No status or process code means a theorem is accepted by LOGOS. Invalid
+elaboration returns no recovered declaration/body as usable evidence, even if
+Lean internally introduced a placeholder to continue reporting errors.
+
+## Structured Name and universe Level
+
+Name is one of:
+
+- `{"tag":"anonymous"}`
+- `{"tag":"str","prefix":<Name>,"value":<string>}`
+- `{"tag":"num","prefix":<Name>,"value":<canonical decimal Nat string>}`
+
+Universe Level is one of:
+
+- `{"tag":"zero"}`
+- `{"tag":"succ","child":<Level>}`
+- `{"tag":"max","left":<Level>,"right":<Level>}`
+- `{"tag":"imax","left":<Level>,"right":<Level>}`
+- `{"tag":"param","name":<Name>}`
+- `{"tag":"mvar","name":<Name>}`
+
+Natural-number values originating in Lean use canonical decimal strings, not
+floating JSON numbers or a machine-width cast. Node references and the bounded
+source byte count are JSON integers. No structural Name is reconstructed from
+`displayName`; display names are non-authoritative labels.
+
+## Core nodes
+
+Every node has `id`, a positive JSON integer equal to its one-based array position,
+and a raw Lean `tag`. Every child ID precedes its parent. The exporter currently
+emits a postorder tree without deduplication; this is a valid DAG ordering, not a
+claim to preserve Lean's internal pointer sharing. Type and value roots use the
+same node array. Constructor-specific keys are exact:
+
+| `tag` | Additional keys |
+|---|---|
+| `bvar` | `index`: canonical decimal Nat string, zero-based de Bruijn index |
+| `fvar`, `mvar` | `name`: structured identifier Name |
+| `sort` | `level`: complete Level |
+| `const` | `name`: Name; `levels`: array of Levels |
+| `app` | `function`, `argument`: child IDs |
+| `lam`, `forallE` | `binderName`: Name; `binderInfo`: `default`, `implicit`, `strictImplicit`, or `instImplicit`; `domain`, `body`: child IDs |
+| `letE` | `binderName`: Name; `type`, `value`, `body`: child IDs; `nondep`: Boolean |
+| `lit` | `kind`: `nat` or `string`; `value`: decimal Nat string or the literal string |
+| `mdata` | `child`: child ID; `entries`: ordered array of `{key:<Name>,value:<DataValue>}` |
+| `proj` | `typeName`: Name; `index`: decimal Nat string; `structure`: child ID |
+
+DataValue has `tag` and `value`: tags `string`, `bool`, `name`, `nat`, `int` retain
+their corresponding primitive or structured value. The `syntaxRepr` tag retains
+Lean4.31's opaque `reprStr Syntax` as a string. This is **not** a stable typed Syntax
+interchange, and no consumer may infer that such metadata was validated or can be
+dropped. The initial LOGOS slice rejects `mdata` and other unsupported constructors
+explicitly; a future Syntax/metadata mapping needs its own contract and tests.
+
+LOGOS chooses the semantic mapping: `sort` with Level `zero` becomes target Prop;
+`bvar` becomes a scoped variable; binder nodes manage lexical scope. A host may
+extract raw tags/primitive child slots but cannot perform those semantic mappings
+or narrow arbitrary Nat strings to machine integers without LOGOS validation.
+
+## Declaration closure
+
+Each declaration has exactly `name`, `displayName`, `kind`, `levelParams`,
+`typeRoot`, `valueRoot`, and `details`. `typeRoot` is a positive node ID; `valueRoot`
+is a positive node ID or null. The requested target's kind must be checked by
+LOGOS. The exporter also retains non-theorem declarations needed by the closure.
+
+| `kind` | Exact `details` keys |
+|---|---|
+| `axiom` | `isUnsafe` Boolean |
+| `definition` | `hints`, `safety`, `all` |
+| `theorem` | `all` |
+| `opaque` | `isUnsafe`, `all` |
+| `quotient` | `kind`: `type`, `ctor`, `lift`, or `ind` |
+| `inductive` | `numParams`, `numIndices`, `all`, `ctors`, `numNested`, `isRec`, `isUnsafe`, `isReflexive` |
+| `constructor` | `induct`, `index`, `numParams`, `numFields`, `isUnsafe` |
+| `recursor` | `all`, `numParams`, `numIndices`, `numMotives`, `numMinors`, `rules`, `k`, `isUnsafe` |
+
+`all` and `ctors` contain Names. `induct` is a Name. Counts/indices use decimal Nat
+strings. Recursor rules have `ctor` Name, `numFields` decimal string and `rhsRoot`
+node ID. Safety is `safe`, `unsafe`, or `partial`. Hints have `tag` of `opaque` or
+`abbrev`, or `tag=regular` with a decimal-string `height`. Booleans are actual JSON
+Booleans. Closure traversal follows constants/projection type names in all emitted
+expressions plus retained mutual-block/constructor/recursor references. Missing
+checked declarations fail, rather than disappearing from the export.
+
+These facts preserve evidence; they do not implement whole-project translation,
+inductive reconstruction, or a validated target-kernel environment importer.
+
+## Explicit trusted-evaluation budgets
+
+Current source constants bound source to 32768 UTF-8 bytes, expression depth to
+512, expanded core nodes to 4096, total expanded universe nodes to 4096 and
+declaration closure to 32 entries. Diagnostics are at most 32 messages of 4096
+bytes each. Final JSON plus newline is at most 262144 bytes. Resource exhaustion
+is failure, not an unsupported-mathematics verdict. The larger output allowance
+is needed for the long fixture; **the existing 32768-byte `proof_process` port is
+not changed or silently reused beyond its limit.** The outer runner must supply
+its separately explicit wall-time, memory, file and captured-output bounds.
+
+`long_composition` is generated by Lean itself from a fixed header, exactly 256
+`apply step` tactic lines, and `exact initial`. Its exact generated source bytes
+are included in the export. This tests an actual long elaborated application
+chain, not comments/padding or manually manufactured core nodes. It is a regression
+example, not a maximum product theorem size or evidence of complete Lean coverage.
+
+## Commands after the exclusive test slot is granted
+
+Use the exact installed Lean4.31 compiler identified in the task evidence, not
+an elan default, a PATH alias or a downloaded latest compiler. Compile the trusted
+library to an owned temporary module directory:
+
+```text
+lean -o <owned-module-directory>/CoreExport.olean <absolute-source-directory>/CoreExport.lean
+LEAN_PATH=<owned-module-directory> lean --run <absolute-source-directory>/Export.lean identity
+LEAN_PATH=<owned-module-directory> lean --run <absolute-source-directory>/Tests.lean identity
+```
+
+The `LEAN_PATH=...` spelling is schematic environment assignment, not a PowerShell
+command. Do not modify user/system environment variables. `Export.lean` accepts
+`identity`, `composition`, `repeated_binders`, `long_composition`, `invalid_proof`,
+`sorry`, or `axiom_dependency`. Actual process-level exit-code, byte-identity,
+determinism, malformed-JSON and LOGOS acceptance checks belong to the outer real
+adapter tier. The direct Lean tests are not a substitute for that tier.
+
+Run `Tests.lean` once per fixture ID above, plus `unknown_source`: eight owned,
+serial process invocations. Each of the four valid theorem checks performs two
+exports for determinism. One monolithic interpreter run accumulated imported
+environments beyond the explicit 4 GiB working-set ceiling; separating processes
+preserves the same tests and budgets. Current supervision is 120 seconds, 4 GiB
+sampled working set and 1 MiB captured stdout/stderr per owned process. This is
+cooperative bounded evaluation, not a kernel-enforced memory sandbox.
+
+Known remaining classification risk: a Lean elaborator heartbeat/depth quota
+reported through `MessageLog.hasErrors` currently follows `elaboration-error`.
+This has not been exercised or proven to preserve a distinct resource-limit
+status; it must be resolved before treating that status as mathematical invalidity
+for arbitrary source. Lean 4.31 is an exact installed evaluation pin, not a claim
+that it has received later compiler/kernel security fixes or production admission.
